steps.body:
Before you can style anything, the browser has to find your CSS. There are three places to put it, and one of them is right for almost everything. Then there's a stylesheet you didn't write, which is already styling every page you make.

## Three places for CSS

- **A stylesheet file**, linked from the head with `<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/style.css">`. One file can style every page of a site, and the browser keeps a copy, so the second page loads faster. This is where your CSS should go.
- **A `<style>` element** in the head holds CSS for that one page. It's handy for trying things out, and for tiny pages like the examples in the previous lessons.
- **A `style` attribute** on one element, like `<p style="color: crimson">`, styles that element alone. It mixes looks into the content, can't be shared, and beats nearly every other rule, which makes it hard to override later. Leave it to scripts that change one element's style on the fly.

## The parts of a rule

You met rules in the lesson on how a browser draws a page. Here are their parts by name:

```css
/* The menu's prices */
.price {
  color: crimson;
  font-weight: bold;
}
```

- `.price` is the **selector**: which elements the rule applies to.
- The curly braces hold the **declaration block**.
- Each line inside is a **declaration**: a **property**, a colon, and a **value**, with a semicolon after it to end it. The semicolon after the last one is optional, but always writing it means adding a line never breaks the one above.
- `/*` and `*/` wrap a **comment**, a note the browser ignores.

When a declaration has a mistake, like a misspelled property or a value that property doesn't take, the browser skips that one declaration and carries on with the rest. It never tells you, except in DevTools, so a typo looks like a rule that does nothing.

## The user agent stylesheet, and resets

Every browser applies its own stylesheet first, the user agent stylesheet from the previous lessons. It gives headings their size, paragraphs their space, lists their bullets, and the `body` a margin of 8 pixels. Each browser's differs a little.

Most projects start their CSS with a **reset**: a few rules that undo the defaults that get in the way, so the page starts from the same place in every browser. Big resets once wiped out every default. Modern ones are small:

```css
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body {
  margin: 0;
}

img {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}

button, input, select, textarea {
  font: inherit;
}
```

The first rule changes how widths are measured, which the box model part explains. The second removes the body's margin, the third stops pictures from spilling out of narrow screens, and the last makes form fields use the page's font instead of the browser's own small one. Later parts explain each piece.

## Watching your CSS in DevTools

DevTools shows you exactly which rules apply to an element, and why. In the Elements tab, click an element, and look at the panes beside the tree:

- **Styles** lists every rule that matches the element, the winning ones at the top, each with its file and line number. A declaration that lost to another is struck through. One the browser couldn't understand has a warning sign. Rules marked user agent stylesheet are the browser's own. You can click any value and change it, or untick a declaration to turn it off, and the page updates at once. Nothing is saved: reload and it's gone.
- **Computed** shows the final value of every property for that element, the computed style from the lesson on how a browser draws a page. Click the arrow by a property to see which rule set it.

Whenever a rule doesn't seem to work, this is the first place to look. It tells you whether the rule matched at all, and if it did, which rule beat it.

## When a change doesn't show

Browsers keep copies of files to load pages faster, and sometimes they show you yesterday's stylesheet. If you save a change and reload but nothing changes, do a **hard reload**, which fetches every file again: press Ctrl, Shift, and R, or Command, Shift, and R on a Mac. Or, while DevTools is open, tick **Disable cache** in its Network panel, and every reload fetches fresh files.

examples.code:
body {
  font-family: Georgia, serif;
  line-height: 1.5;
  padding: 16px;
}

h1 {
  color: saddlebrown;
}

h2 {
  colour: saddlebrown;
}

nav ul {
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
}

nav li {
  display: inline;
}

nav li + li::before {
  content: " | ";
}

nav a:hover {
  color: saddlebrown;
}

a:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid darkorange;
}

details[open] summary {
  font-weight: bold;
}

main:has(details[open]) h1 {
  color: darkgreen;
}

steps.body:
Not every value comes from a rule that picks the element. Some flow down from its parent, which is why setting one font on the `body` changes the whole page. That same flow lets you define a color once and use it everywhere, and change it everywhere at once.

## What's inherited

**Inheritance** passes a property's value from an element to its children, when nothing sets it for them. Properties about text are inherited: `color`, `font-family`, `font-size`, `font-weight`, `line-height`, `text-align`, and `visibility`. So one rule on the `body` sets the font for every paragraph, heading, and list on the page.

Properties about boxes aren't: `margin`, `padding`, `border`, `background`, `width`, `height`, and `display`. Imagine if they were: a border on the `body` would put a border around every element on the page. When a property isn't inherited and nothing sets it, it takes its **initial value**, the default written in the CSS standard, like `0` for margins.

Not every element joins in. Browsers give form fields and buttons their own font in the user agent stylesheet, so they don't inherit yours. That's why the reset from the first part says `font: inherit` for them.

## Keywords for every property

Four values work on any property:

- `inherit` takes the parent's value, even for a property that isn't normally inherited.
- `initial` takes the initial value from the standard.
- `unset` acts as `inherit` for inherited properties, and as `initial` for the rest.
- `revert` goes back to what the browser's own stylesheet would give it, as if your rules weren't there.

## Custom properties

A **custom property** is a value you name and reuse. Its name starts with two dashes, and you read it with `var()`:

```css
:root {
  --brand: saddlebrown;
  --space: 16px;
}

h1 {
  color: var(--brand);
  margin-bottom: var(--space);
}
```

`:root` is a pseudo-class that picks the `html` element, the root of the tree. Define the bakery's brown there once, use `var(--brand)` everywhere, and changing that one line repaints the whole site. Values like these, which hold a design's colors and sizes, are often called **design tokens**.

Custom properties are inherited, which makes them more useful still: setting one on an element changes it for everything inside. `.sale { --brand: crimson; }` turns the brand color red inside any element with the class `sale`, and nowhere else. The lesson on responsive CSS builds light and dark themes this way.

`var()` can take a fallback after a comma, used when the property isn't set at all: `var(--accent, darkorange)`.

## Nesting

Stylesheets repeat themselves. A card and the things inside it need a rule each, and each starts with `.card`. **Nesting** lets you write rules inside other rules:

```css
.card {
  padding: var(--space);

  & h2 {
    color: var(--brand);
  }

  &:hover {
    background: oldlace;
  }
}
```

`&` stands for the outer selector, so `& h2` means `.card h2`, and `&:hover` means `.card:hover`. With no space after `&`, the two join, as in `&.featured`, which means `.card.featured`. Writing `&` is optional when the inner selector is separated by a space, but writing it always keeps things clear. Nesting is Baseline Widely available, so it works without any tools.

Keep it shallow. Each level adds to the selector's specificity, as if you'd written it out in full, and three levels deep is already hard to read.

